## Context

EVM-native staking mints sUSDr immediately, while the USDr backing and share accounting settle on Cardano later. During a rising v1.1 diffusion window, the shares accrue a bounded catch-up liability while the route is in flight.

The agreed protocol treatment is documented in the [PR #21 follow-up](https://github.com/realfi-co/realfi-cross-chain-spine/pull/21#issuecomment-4967047449): Cardano uses undistributed pending yield as the catch-up reserve, and EVM must admit native stakes only while that reserve can safely cover every accepted in-flight stake. EVM-native staking is paused and drained before the next diffusion window is installed.

This issue tracks the EVM-side work required to support that policy. Cardano accounting and lock-step target reset are tracked separately.

## Required EVM behavior

### 1. Receive an enforceable staking epoch configuration

EVM needs authenticated state derived from the active Cardano diffusion window, sufficient to enforce native-stake admission before minting. At minimum:

- staking epoch / rate-sequence identifier;
- current rate or the parameters required to reproduce the current interpolated rate;
- native-staking quota for the epoch;
- maximum individual native stake, if configured;
- native-staking cutoff time/block;
- paused/unpaused state;
- freshness information.

Decide whether these fields extend RateUpdate or use a dedicated signed configuration message. Applying a new epoch must reset cumulative quota usage only after the previous epoch has been paused and drained on Cardano.

The contract must fail closed when configuration is stale, missing, out of order, expired, or paused.

### 2. Enforce admission before immediate minting

Before burning/relocating USDr or minting sUSDr, the EVM staking entry contract must:

- reproduce the prevailing interpolated rate;
- reject after the configured cutoff;
- reject while paused;
- reject an amount over the per-stake limit;
- calculate the exact sUSDr shares using the protocol rounding rule;
- reject if the stake would exceed the remaining epoch quota;
- increment cumulative epoch usage atomically with the mint.

The quota represents Cardano-authorized catch-up capacity. Because sUSDr is already minted when StakeRoute is emitted, this check cannot be deferred to the spine or attestors.

A cumulative per-epoch quota is preferred initially: accepted capacity is not released on individual Cardano settlement, and resets only with the next authenticated epoch after the pause/drain barrier.

### 3. Commit the exact pricing decision in StakeRoute

Extend the emitted StakeRoute payload to commit enough information for Cardano to reproduce the EVM calculation:

- exact sUSDr shares minted;
- rate sequence / staking epoch used;
- authenticated EVM origin time or block reference used for interpolation;
- any finalized schema/version tag required for forward compatibility.

The EVM event and RFC1 message must commit the same values. Cardano must be able to reject a mismatch between the committed shares and the historical rate.

Finalize the hookData schema and add authoritative EVM-generated conformance vectors.

### 4. Support the pause/drain lifecycle

Provide an authenticated control path that can:

1. announce the cutoff for the current epoch;
2. pause new EVM-native stakes;
3. leave normal bridge transfers operational;
4. keep the bridge-to-Cardano → stake → bridge-back route available;
5. install the next epoch only after Cardano reports the previous cutoff drained;
6. unpause native staking after the new configuration is finalized on EVM.

Emergency pause must remain available. Unpausing and quota reset must be protected against replay and out-of-order configuration messages.

### 5. Expose operational state

Emit/index events and expose read methods for:

- active epoch and rate sequence;
- native-staking quota and cumulative usage;
- remaining quota;
- cutoff;
- pause reason/state;
- accepted stake amount, shares, effective rate/time, and epoch.

This state is needed by the UI, monitoring, spine reconciliation, and incident response.

## Failure behavior

- Stale rate/configuration: native staking reverts.
- Quota exhausted: native staking reverts and UI directs users to the Cardano route.
- Cutoff reached: native staking reverts.
- Drain failure: native staking remains paused; no local timeout may unpause it.
- Duplicate or older epoch update: rejected/no state regression.
- Portfolio loss/rate decrease: no catch-up reservation is required, but the same authenticated epoch and quota rules still apply.

Normal token bridging must not be disabled merely because native staking is paused.

## Tests

- Exact interpolation and floor rounding match Cardano vectors.
- Stake succeeds within quota and increments usage atomically.
- Stake exceeding quota or per-stake limit reverts before mint/burn.
- Stake at/after cutoff reverts.
- Stale, paused, replayed, and out-of-order configurations revert.
- New epoch resets usage exactly once.
- StakeRoute commits shares, epoch/rate sequence, and origin time/block correctly.
- Multiple stakes cannot race past the quota.
- Emergency pause and failed-drain state remain fail-closed.
- Normal bridge-to-Cardano flows continue while native staking is paused.
- End-to-end vector: EVM stake → StakeRoute → Cardano catch-up deduction from pending yield.

## Acceptance criteria

- Immediate EVM minting can occur only against Cardano-authorized catch-up capacity.
- Every accepted stake is deterministically reproducible on Cardano.
- EVM-native staking is guaranteed paused across the cutoff/drain/target-reset boundary.
- Quota exhaustion or stale state cannot create already-minted but unserviceable shares.
- Users retain the Cardano staking route while EVM-native staking is paused.
- EVM, Cardano, and spine conformance vectors agree byte-for-byte.
